From dcf686c8b65d92b203870d9e2ad4347cc92f97aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: GloriousEggroll <gloriouseggroll@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 15:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] tiling: report window places and add PlaceOnOutput

The windows reply of the tiling control socket gains, per entry and after
the existing keys: app_id (what <windowRule identifier=""> matches),
title, type_normal (whether type="normal" would match), output_name,
maximized and place. place is the window's position relative to the
top-left corner of its output plus its size, in the same units as the
existing x/y/width/height (the client area without server-side
decorations, which for client-decorated windows is their window
geometry). For maximized, fullscreen, snapped or tiled windows it is the
geometry they return to when restored, when that is known on the same
output. Strings are JSON-escaped, invalid UTF-8 becomes U+FFFD, app_id is
cut to 128 bytes and title to 96 bytes on a character boundary. A reply
that would exceed the 60000-byte limit is sent again with empty titles.
Every reply ends with "positions_supported":true.

The PlaceOnOutput action moves a window to the named output (connector
name, case-insensitive) and gives it the position and size the place of
the windows reply describes, so that feeding place back into the action
puts a window at the same spot. Fullscreen, maximized, snapped and shaded
states are left first, the frame is shrunk and shifted into the usable
area, a width or height of 0 keeps the window's size, and maximized="yes"
maximizes it on that output afterwards. An unknown or unusable output
leaves the window alone. A window that tiling arranges, or will arrange,
is only moved to the output and tiling places it.

As a window rule it runs on first map, after labwc's initial placement
(xdg-shell windows are placed in the map handler just before the rules
run, XWayland windows when they are associated), so the rule wins; the
size of client-decorated windows is known by then.
